WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.620
Film Courage: So let’s suppose…let’s
have it be one [project that Christine is

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.740
working on] because I’m really curious how
you spend your time and how you organize [your

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:07.740
day].

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.480
If you have 4 weeks to write a first draft
for one [screenplay], what’s your work schedule

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.480
going to be like?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.340
Christine Conradt: My process is a little
different than most people.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.490
I write 15 pages a day.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:24.880
So basically I figure out when it’s due
and I back out 15 pages per day and I take

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.690
into consideration if I’ve got something
going on in the afternoon.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.630
Like today is not a 15 page day because I’m
with you guys.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.059
Film Courage: Thank you!

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:34.640
Christine Conradt: And then I work until those
15 pages are done.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.860
So if I’m done at 3:00 o’clock because
I had a great writing day and it went really

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.730
smoothly fantastic.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:42.860
If it takes me until 9:00 o’clock, it takes
me until 9:00 o’clock and then that’s

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.700
when I end my day.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.010
It helps me to do that because I keep on track
and I also I don’t get caught up in my own

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.010
“Oh my gosh!

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.010
I’m loving this.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.010
I just want to keep going.”

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.790
Because that can happen to, when you’re
really on a role writing you will…I mean

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:01.219
I’ve had that happen where you don’t eat
and you just keep working through the night

00:01:01.219 --> 00:01:03.150
and you’re just so excited about what you’re
writing.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.569
That’s fine but you can’t make a living
living that way, right?

00:01:07.569 --> 00:01:12.850
You have relationships and I have people and
dinner to make and things like that and so

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:18.649
I stop when I (this is my way of doing it),
I stop at 15 pages and that’s it and pick

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:20.619
back up tomorrow and start again.

00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:21.710
Film Courage: Wow.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.659
Were you always so precise with it?

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:24.659
I mean that’s amazing.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:25.659
Christine Conradt: Yeah.

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:29.939
It kind of feels very…I know that seems
very technical, for something that is so creative.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:34.780
But again, like I said before, you have to
look at this like a business, right?

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.659
So you have to plan and organize and figure
out what’s giving you the best results and

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:42.649
for me, this gave me the best results.

00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:47.219
Because I know at page 11, I only have a few
more pages to do and I can be done.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:52.170
And at the beginning of every day I start
out and I go “Okay, that’s enough.”

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.259
It’s a big enough chunk that I’m not forcing
myself to go back and reread.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:01:59.950
That’s a deadly thing that writers do is
they go back and they rewrite themselves over

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:00.950
and over.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.499
So it forces you to keep moving through and
then you do your rewrite at the end.

00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:08.039
Film Courage: So once those 110 pages are
done (or whatever), that’s when you go back?

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:09.039
Christine Conradt: That’s when I go back.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:14.380
And I build in a couple of days off because
you need that time away from it to kind of

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.250
forget about it a little bit and then when
I come back, I reread it.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.569
I give myself 2 or 3 days to do the rewrite
and it’s basically just cleaning everything

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:26.430
up or changing act breaks or if I’ve come
up with an idea in between that time to add,

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.879
then I’ll do that.

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:31.980
Film Courage: That’s great because you want
to think that you are your own boss but really

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.879
the network is your boss.

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:37.800
You’re working for them but you’ve also
got to monitor yourself.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.489
That sounds like a great way to keep yourself
on track so you’re not doing 2 pages a day,

00:02:43.489 --> 00:02:45.150
but you are not burning yourself out.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.150
Christine Conradt: Right.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.319
And I think also think you can get obsessed
with “Is the writing good?

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:50.319
Is it not good?

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:51.319
Do I like this?

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:52.319
Do I not like it?”

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:56.040
When you have to get those 15 pages done,
eventually you have to say “Darn it.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.409
I’ve got to move on."

00:02:57.409 --> 00:02:59.019
It forces you to move on.

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:04.629
Which I think is good for the process because
nothing really good comes from sitting there

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:07.010
obsessing over the same scene.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.249
So, it is what it is.

00:03:09.249 --> 00:03:10.249
I’ve moved on.

00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:14.120
I can come back to it and giving yourself
permission to do that I think is very healthy

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.499
and I think it’s actually good for the process
and the product.

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:21.200
Film Courage: Let’s suppose you have 3 projects
going.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.590
Are you still doing 15 pages [a day]?

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.680
Christine Conradt: It depends.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.959
So…yes, 15 pages on something.

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:31.620
Some writers will say I’m going to do 7
pages on this project in the morning and then

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.620
7 pages later.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.620
I don’t do that.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:38.439
I say “Okay, I’m doing 15 pages on this
and taking 3 days off to work on the next

00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:40.749
project and then I come back to it.

00:03:40.749 --> 00:03:42.909
So that’s how I organize it.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:47.909
I have a hard time splitting it up and trying
to work on different multiple projects in

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:50.139
the same day, especially at 15 pages.

00:03:50.139 --> 00:03:54.049
I think if you were doing fewer maybe, but
15 is pretty ambitious.

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:58.569
Film Courage: Yeah, that sounds difficult
if the stories are so different or you just

00:03:58.569 --> 00:04:02.700
want to keep the character and all of that
separate and to have to split it up in one

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.700
day for your mind.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.840
Christine Conradt: It’s worse when they
are actually similar.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.709
Like if they are both projects for the same
network or they are both thrillers because

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:11.709
that’s when it gets confusing.

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:16.680
It’s actually better when you are doing
like a romantic comedy and then also working

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.089
on a thriller because they’re so different,
you don’t mix those characters up at all.

